
the incredible   *** life ***   program

this program runs in the gt40 and simulates
conway's "life" cellular automaton.

the first section describes the game itself for those among us
not familiar with it.  the second section describes the
implementation and how to play it.

1:	the specifications are very simple:

it is played on a theoretically infinite board
and simulates bacteria cells growing and dying.

to produce the next generation:

a cell with less than 2 or more than 3 active neighbours will die
(or stay dead).
a cell with exactly 2 neighbours will remain in its current state.
a cell with excatly 3 neighbours will be alive next time.

note that it is not really a "game" in that there is no well defined
object, unless looking for particular patterns is your interest.

2:	the implementation

it is played on a 32*32 board where the left & right hand edges join
as do the top & bottom.

to set up:

the board is set up by placing the cursor over a particular cell
and pressing 'a' to activate that cell, or 'd' to delete it.
the cursor may be moved by the keys on the left (including "home"
which goes to top left hand corner, and 'c' which goes to
the centre).  the light pen may also be used.

dead cells are shown as "+", alive ones by 'o'.  when running, dead
cells are shown as blanks.

the digits 1-8 may also be used, and move the cursor to the
appropriate neighbour as shown:

	1!2!3
	-----
	8!x!4
	-----
	7!6!5

these keys may be repeated as often as required.

whole blocks, rows or columns may be activated or deleted by
the 'b' key.  position the cursor to one spot, press 'b' and that
place is "remembered".  move the cursor elsewhere and press
'a' or 'd' for the desired operation.  note that rows and columns
are the same as blocks.
also, the border only may be generated by using 'o' rather than 'b'.

when you have finished setting up, press 'g' (for go) or 'r'.
the display then blanks out all dead cells.  pressing the
's' key (for single step) will result in one gen. at a time
being displayed.
pressing 'r' or 'g' will display continuously about every 1/2 sec.
pressing 'h' for halt will stop it.

the display can be reinitialised by pressing 'rubout',
and it can be modified in mid display by pressing 'm' (for
modify), then using the light pen or cursor keys as initialising.

3:	summary

initialising/modifying:
	a	activate cell
	d	delete cell
	c	go to centre of screen
	b	block mode
	o	outline mode
	g	go (start generating)
	r	same as 'g'
	home	go to top left corner
	1-8	go to appropriate neighbour
	arrows	move in appropriate direction
	rubout	clear patterns and start again

running:
	r	run
	g	same as 'r'
	s	single step
	h	halt
	rubout	reinitialise
	m	modify

it is loaded by using the command:  gtl life
(after the usual sequence
halt; load address 166000; enable; start)

report any glitches to dave horsfall, rm 1408.
